      <memberdef kind="function" id="class_f_tryll_managed_server_process_1adb8625906bb159c31dbd4a5975a7bc12" prot="public" static="no" const="no" explicit="no" inline="no" virt="non-virtual">
        <type>bool</type>
        <definition>bool FTryllManagedServerProcess::Spawn</definition>
        <argsstring>(const FString &amp;ExePath, int32 Port, int32 IdleShutdownTimeoutSeconds, FString &amp;OutError, bool &amp;bOutPortConflict)</argsstring>
        <name>Spawn</name>
        <qualifiedname>FTryllManagedServerProcess::Spawn</qualifiedname>
        <param>
          <type>const FString &amp;</type>
          <declname>ExePath</declname>
        </param>
        <param>
          <type>int32</type>
          <declname>Port</declname>
        </param>
        <param>
          <type>int32</type>
          <declname>IdleShutdownTimeoutSeconds</declname>
        </param>
        <param>
          <type>FString &amp;</type>
          <declname>OutError</declname>
        </param>
        <param>
          <type>bool &amp;</type>
          <declname>bOutPortConflict</declname>
        </param>
        <briefdescription>
        </briefdescription>
        <detaileddescription>
<para>Spawns ExePath with &quot;--port Port --idle-shutdown-timeout Seconds&quot; and blocks until its TCP port accepts a connection or a bounded readiness timeout elapses — without this, callers would report launch success as soon as the OS process handle exists, even though cold startup (config/model-catalog loading) or a port collision can leave the port unreachable, or unreachable forever if the process already exited.</para>
<para>On failure, OutError is populated with a clear reason. bOutPortConflict is set to true iff the process exited with Tryll::ServerExitPortInUse (3), so callers can special-case a bind collision (retry on a different port) versus any other failure. </para>
        </detaileddescription>
        <inbodydescription>
        </inbodydescription>
